'''Matsumen Kofun''' (松面古墳) was a {{ill|Square burial mound|ja|方墳}} located in {{ill|Chiba Prefecture|ja|千葉県}}, {{ill|Kisarazu|ja|木更津市}}, Asahi. It was part of the {{ill|Gion-Nagasuka Kofun Cluster|ja|祇園・長須賀古墳群}}, but its mound has since disappeared.

This article also discusses the nearby '''{{ill|Tsuka-no-Koshi Kofun|ja|塚の腰古墳}}''', located south of Matsumen Kofun.

== Overview ==

Matsumen Kofun was a large square tomb constructed on the left bank of the alluvial plains of the {{ill|Obitsu River|ja|小櫃川}} in central Chiba Prefecture. South of the tomb lay a large ditch (No. 2 ditch), beyond which {{ill|Tsuka-no-Koshi Kofun|ja|塚の腰古墳}} was located. Excavations in {{ill|1938|ja|昭和13年}} revealed burial chambers and grave goods, followed by additional surveys in 2005, 2010, and 2014.

The tomb, previously thought to be a small round mound, measured 44.2m east-to-west and 45.3m north-to-south, making it the third-largest square tomb in Chiba Prefecture. Surrounding the mound were moats and an outer ditch, encompassing 69.5m and 84.5m respectively. Artifacts such as wooden tools (plow blades, shafts, stakes, and sledges) were recovered from the moats. Notably, a {{ill|sledge|ja|修羅}} used to transport stones during tomb construction was the second of its kind discovered in Japan, following the one from {{ill|Mitsuzuka Kofun|ja|三ツ塚古墳}} in {{ill|Osaka Prefecture|ja|大阪府}}. The burial chambers, constructed with cut soft sandstone and paved stone floors, were of the dual-sleeved horizontal stone chamber type. A sarcophagus was possibly used but remains unconfirmed.

Artifacts discovered during the {{ill|1938|ja|昭和13年}} excavation included decorated swords, weapons, ornaments, horse tack, {{ill|Sue ware|ja|須恵器}}, and {{ill|Haji ware|ja|土師器}}.

The tomb dates to the terminal {{ill|Kofun period|ja|古墳時代}}, approximately the early {{ill|7th century|ja|7世紀}} (TK209 typology). Within the {{ill|Gion-Nagasuka Kofun Cluster|ja|祇園・長須賀古墳群}}, it succeeded the {{ill|Kinrei Kofun|ja|金鈴塚古墳}} and preceded {{ill|Tsuka-no-Koshi Kofun|ja|塚の腰古墳}} as the burial site of a local chief, and it is considered one of the most significant terminal Kofun period tombs in the Bōsō region.

== History of Investigations ==

* {{ill|1909|ja|明治42年}}: Excavation of {{ill|Tsuka-no-Koshi Kofun|ja|塚の腰古墳}} during land reclamation or roadwork (recorded in the "Kimitsu District Gazetteer," "Nagasuka Local History," and "Kisarazu City History"). {{Sfn|Matsumen Kofun Excavation Report|2016}}
** Artifacts: Initially kept by the landowner, donated to the Chiba Prefectural Kazusa Museum in {{ill|1972|ja|昭和47年}} and currently housed at the {{ill|Kisarazu City Folk Museum Kin-no-Suzu|ja|木更津市郷土博物館金のすず}}. {{Sfn|Matsumen Kofun Excavation Report|2016}}
* {{ill|1938|ja|昭和13年}}: Emergency survey during construction of Kimitsu Hospital; tomb leveled after investigation (Sugō Site Research Group). {{Sfn|Gion-Nagasuka Kofun Cluster Historical Records|2003}}
** Artifacts discovered on June 29 were purchased by the {{ill|Tokyo National Museum|ja|東京国立博物館}} via the Kisarazu Police and Chiba Prefecture. {{Sfn|Matsumen Kofun Excavation Report|2016}}
** Artifacts unearthed on July 19–20 were transferred to {{ill|Kokugakuin University|ja|國學院大學}}. {{Sfn|Matsumen Kofun Excavation Report|2016}}
* {{ill|2005|ja|平成17年}}: Confirmation survey during soil improvement and removal work (reported in {{ill|2006|ja|平成18年}}). {{Sfn|Matsumen Kofun Excavation Report|2016}}
* {{ill|2010|ja|平成22年}}: Further confirmation survey (reported in {{ill|2013|ja|平成25年}}). {{Sfn|Matsumen Kofun Excavation Report|2016}}
* {{ill|2014|ja|平成26年}}: Full excavation conducted (report published in {{ill|2016|ja|平成28年}}). {{Sfn|Matsumen Kofun Excavation Report|2016}}

== Artifacts ==

[[File:Matsumen Kofun Sword.jpg|thumb|250px|right|{{center|Twin-Dragon Ring Sword}}{{small|Exhibited at the {{ill|Tokyo National Museum|ja|東京国立博物館}}.}}]]
[[File:Matsumen Kofun Ornament.jpg|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Gold-copper Fish Pendants}}{{small|Exhibited at the Tokyo National Museum.}}]]
[[File:Matsumen Kofun Sue Ware.jpg|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Sue Ware Pottery}}{{small|Exhibited at the Tokyo National Museum.}}]]
Artifacts excavated in {{ill|1938|ja|昭和13年}} include: {{Sfn|Matsumen Kofun Excavation Report|2016}}
* Swords
  * Gold-copper twin-dragon ring sword (T)
  * Gold-copper sword (location unknown)
  * Silver pommel sword hilt (T)
  * Gold-copper sheath fittings (T)
  * Wooden club pommel (location unknown)
* Weapons
  * Straight swords (two or more) (T)
  * Iron halberd blade and butt end (K)
  * Silver pommel (possibly K)
  * Iron arrowheads (T, K)
* Ornaments
  * Gold-copper fish pendants (T)
  * Gold-copper glass-decorated leaf pendants (K)
  * Gold-copper floral fittings (K)
  * Glass beads (associated with leaf pendants)
* Horse tack
  * Gold-copper bracketed heart-shaped pendants (T)
  * Gold-copper saddle fittings (T1, K2)
  * Gold-copper hoops (T)
  * Iron stirrups (T)
  * Iron-copper riveted fasteners (T)
* Copper bowl (K)
* {{ill|Sue ware|ja|須恵器}}: Lidded cups, high-necked jars, pots (T)
* {{ill|Haji ware|ja|土師器}}: Bowls and jars (T)

== Related Facilities ==

* {{ill|Kisarazu City Folk Museum Kin-no-Suzu|ja|木更津市郷土博物館金のすず}} (Kisarazu City) - Displays artifacts from {{ill|Matsumen Kofun|ja|松面古墳}} and {{ill|Tsuka-no-Koshi Kofun|ja|塚の腰古墳}}.
* {{ill|Tokyo National Museum|ja|東京国立博物館}} (Tokyo) - Stores artifacts from Matsumen Kofun.
* {{ill|Kokugakuin University Museum|ja|國學院大學博物館}} (Tokyo) - Stores artifacts from Matsumen Kofun (not regularly exhibited).

