'''Matsumen Kofun''' (松面古墳) was a Kofun (kofun) located in Asahi, {{ill|Chiba Prefecture|ja|千葉県|12=draft}}{{ill|Kisarazu City|ja|木更津市|12=draft}}. Its type was that of a {{ill|Square Kofun|ja|方墳|1=Square Kofun|1=Square Kofun|12=draft}}. It was one of the kofun comprising the {{ill|Gion‐Nagasuka Kofun Group|ja|祇園・長須賀古墳群|12=draft}}. The tumulus is now lost.

In this article the mound located to the south of Matsumen Kofun – '''{{ill|Tsukanokoshi Kofun|ja|塚の腰古墳|12=draft}}''' – is also explained.

== Overview ==
It is a large square kofun built on the alluvial plain along the left bank of the lower reaches of the {{ill|Kobiki River|ja|小櫃川|12=draft}} in central Chiba Prefecture. To the south, separated by a large moat (Moat No.2), stood the Tsukanokoshi Kofun. In {{ill|1938|ja|1938th year|12=draft}} (the 13th year of the {{ill|Showa era|ja|昭和|12=draft}}), its stone chamber was excavated and investigated and burial goods were unearthed; later, after being leveled and becoming a buried mound, excavation surveys were carried out in {{ill|2005|ja|2005th year |lt=2005|12=draft}}, {{ill|2010|ja|2010th year |lt=2010|12=draft}}, and {{ill|2014|ja|2014th year|12=draft}} (in the 17th, 22nd, and 26th fiscal years of the {{ill|Heisei era|ja|平成|12=draft}}).

The tumulus was square in type (previously regarded as a small circular mound before investigation), measuring 44.2 meters east–west and 45.3 meters north–south – making it the third largest square kofun in Chiba Prefecture{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}. Around the mound, both a peripheral moat and an outer moat encircled the site; including the peripheral moat, the overall map_size was approximately 69.5 meters, and including the outer moat, about 84.5 meters{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}. From the peripheral moat were unearthed wooden objects – such as plowshares, handles, stakes, {{ill|Shura|ja|修羅 (そり)|lt=Shura|lt_ja=修羅|12=draft}}, and sword‐like items – and notably the Shura, used for transporting stone during construction, is noteworthy as only the second example in the country after the {{ill|Mitsuzuka Kofun|ja|三ツ塚古墳|12=draft}} (in {{ill|Osaka Prefecture|ja|大阪府|12=draft}}/{{ill|Fujiidera City|ja|藤井寺市|12=draft}}). The burial facility was the two‐sleeve style {{ill|Horizontal tunnel‐style stone chamber|ja|横穴式石室|1=Corridor-Style Stone Chamber|2=ja|3=横穴式石室|4=zh|5=橫穴式石室|6=ko|7=굴식 돌방무덤|1=Corridor-Style Stone Chamber|2=ja|3=横穴式石室|1=Corridor-Style Stone Chamber|2=ja|3=横穴式石室|4=zh|5=橫穴式石室|6=ko|7=굴식 돌방무덤|4=zh|5=橫穴式石室|6=ko|7=굴식 돌방무덤|1=Corridor-Style Stone Chamber|12=draft}}. Its stone chamber was constructed from cut soft sandstone with a flagstone floor, according to reports{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}. Although there is a possibility that a stone coffin passed on to a hospital was used, no other records mention this, leaving it unclear{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}. The 1938 excavation (13th year of the Showa era) unearthed numerous burial goods from within the stone chamber – including the {{ill|Decorated Sword|ja|装飾付大刀|12=draft}}, various weapons, ornaments, horse fittings, {{ill|Sue pottery|ja|須恵器|1=Sue pottery|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Haji pottery|ja|土師器|1=Haji pottery|12=draft}}.

The construction period is estimated to be in the early {{ill|7th century|ja|7世紀|12=draft}} (TK209 period) of the Late Kofun period{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}. Within the {{ill|Gion‐Nagasuka Kofun Group|ja|祇園・長須賀古墳群|12=draft}}, it is thought to have succeeded the {{ill|Kanazurizuka Kofun|ja|金鈴塚古墳|1=Kinreizuka Kofun|12=draft}} (in {{ill|Kisarazu City Nagasuka|ja|木更津市長須賀|12=draft}}) and is assumed to be a chieftain tomb preceding the Tsukanokoshi Kofun – making it one of the representative Late Kofun period tombs in the Bōsō region.

== Archaeological History ==
* In {{ill|1909|ja|1909th year|12=draft}} (42nd year of the {{ill|Meiji era|ja|明治|1=Meiji era|12=draft}}), the Tsukanokoshi Kofun was excavated during land reclamation (or soil removal for a prefectural road) – as recorded in the Chiba Prefecture Kunitsu County Gazette (Volume 1), Nagasuka Local History, and Kisarazu City History{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}.
** excavations: After being retained by the landowner, all items except for the straight sword were donated in {{ill|1972|ja|1972nd year|12=draft}} (47th year of the {{ill|Showa era|ja|昭和|12=draft}}) to the Chiba Prefectural Kazusa Museum, and are now preserved at the {{ill|Kisarazu City Local Museum Kanenosuzu|ja|木更津市郷土博物館金のすず|12=draft}}{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}.
* In {{ill|1938|ja|1938th year|12=draft}} (13th year of the Showa era), an emergency survey of the Matsumen Kofun was conducted in connection with the construction of the former Kunitsu Hospital; the site was leveled after the survey (by the Suga Archaeological Survey Group){{Sfn|祇園・長須賀古墳群（千葉県の歴史 資料編 考古2）|2003}}.
** The manual excavation on June 29 – the burial goods were purchased and preserved by the {{ill|Tokyo National Museum|ja|東京国立博物館|12=draft}} through Kisarazu Police and Chiba Prefecture{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}.
** The items excavated during the survey on July 19–20 were transferred to and preserved at {{ill|Kokugakuin University|ja|國學院大學|12=draft}}{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}.
* In the fiscal year of {{ill|2005|ja|2005th year|12=draft}} (17th fiscal year of the {{ill|Heisei era|ja|平成|12=draft}}), a verification survey of Matsumen Kofun was conducted in connection with soil improvement and the removal of buried objects (by the Kisarazu City Board of Education, reported in {{ill|2006|ja|2006th year|12=draft}}){{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}.
* In the fiscal year of {{ill|2010|ja|2010th year|12=draft}} (22nd fiscal year of the {{ill|Heisei era|ja|平成|12=draft}}), a verification survey of both Matsumen Kofun and Tsukanokoshi Kofun was carried out in connection with soil improvement and the removal of buried objects (by the Kisarazu City Board of Education, reported in {{ill|2013|ja|2013rd year|12=draft}}){{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}.
* In the fiscal year of {{ill|2014|ja|2014th year|12=draft}} (26th fiscal year of the {{ill|Heisei era|ja|平成|12=draft}}), a full-map_size survey of both Matsumen Kofun and Tsukanokoshi Kofun was conducted in connection with soil improvement and the removal of buried objects (by the Kisarazu City Board of Education, with the report published in {{ill|2016|ja|2016th year|12=draft}}){{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}.

