'''Tsukanokoshi Kofun''' (塚の腰古墳) was a Kofun located to the south of Matsumen Kofun. Its type was that of a {{ill|Square Kofun|ja|方墳|1=Square Kofun|1=Square Kofun|12=draft}}. It was one of the kofun comprising the {{ill|Gion‐Nagasuka Kofun Group|ja|祇園・長須賀古墳群|12=draft}}. The tumulus is now lost.

== Overview ==
It is a large square kofun constructed to the south of Matsumen Kofun, separated by a large moat (Moat No.2). It was excavated in {{ill|1909|ja|1909th year|12=draft}} (42nd year of the {{ill|Meiji era|ja|明治|1=Meiji era|12=draft}}), during which burial goods were unearthed, and further excavation surveys were conducted in the fiscal years of {{ill|2005|ja|2005th year |lt=2005|12=draft}}, {{ill|2010|ja|2010th year |lt=2010|12=draft}}, and {{ill|2014|ja|2014th year|12=draft}} (17th, 22nd, and 26th fiscal years of the {{ill|Heisei era|ja|平成|12=draft}}).

The tumulus was square in type (previously considered a small circular mound before investigation), with each side measuring approximately 39.5 meters. Around the mound were both a peripheral moat and an outer moat; including the peripheral moat, the overall map_size was about 61.0 meters, and including the outer moat, about 73.5 meters{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}. As the burial facility has been lost, its details remain unknown{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}. The 1909 excavation (42nd year of the Meiji era) unearthed burial goods including a bronze mirror, a straight sword, iron arrowheads, horse fittings, and {{ill|Sue pottery|ja|須恵器|1=Sue pottery|12=draft}}, among others.

The construction period is estimated to be in the first half of the {{ill|7th century|ja|7世紀|12=draft}} (TK217 period) of the Late Kofun period. Within the {{ill|Gion‐Nagasuka Kofun Group|ja|祇園・長須賀古墳群|12=draft}}, it is assumed to be a chieftain tomb succeeding Matsumen Kofun{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}.

=== Excavated Items ===
[[File:塚の腰古墳 出土品.JPG|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Excavated Items}}{{small|Displayed at {{ill|Kisarazu City Local Museum Kanenosuzu|ja|木更津市郷土博物館金のすず|12=draft}}.}}]]
The burial goods from the 1909 excavation (42nd year of the Meiji era) are as follows{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}:
* Replica Four‐Animal Mirror, 1 – Diameter 10.75 cm, missing about one–third; similar to the mirror unearthed from Toriya‐baja Kofun No.1 (in Iida, Nagano Prefecture)
* Bronze‐made Saddle and Stirrup Fitting, 1
* Straight Sword, 1
* Amber Bead, 1
* Small Glass Beads, 2
* {{ill|Sue pottery|ja|須恵器|1=Sue pottery|12=draft}} – 1 long–necked jar
According to Nagasuka Local History, iron arrowheads are also believed to have been unearthed{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}. Of the above items, all except the straight sword and iron arrowheads were retained by the landowner, then passed to the Chiba Prefectural Kazusa Museum, and are now preserved at the {{ill|Kisarazu City Local Museum Kanenosuzu|ja|木更津市郷土博物館金のすず|12=draft}}{{Sfn|塚の腰古墳・松面古墳発掘調査報告書|2016}}.

== Related Facilities ==
* {{ill|Kisarazu City Local Museum Kanenosuzu|ja|木更津市郷土博物館金のすず|12=draft}} (Ōta, Kisarazu City) – Preserves the burial goods from Tsukanokoshi Kofun.
* {{ill|Tokyo National Museum|ja|東京国立博物館|12=draft}} (Taitō, Tokyo) – Preserves the burial goods from Matsumen Kofun.
* {{ill|Kokugakuin University Museum|ja|國學院大學博物館|12=draft}} (Higashi, Shibuya, Tokyo; located within the Kokugakuin University Shibuya Campus) – Preserves the burial goods from Matsumen Kofun (not on permanent display).

